course: everyday
lesson: 129
title: Video calls and visiting a friend's house
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: On a video call you sit where people can see your face, in a quiet place with good light, you keep still and do not eat while you talk, and you check before turning the camera on others.
explain: Before visiting a friend you ask your parent, and an adult must know where you are and when to collect you.
explain: A good guest follows the hosts' rules, stays in the rooms they are invited into, helps tidy up, and owns up straight away if something breaks.
explain: Being a guest never means doing something dangerous or against your family's rules: you say no and can phone your parents to go home.
explain: A girl in Edinburgh knocks a vase off a shelf at her friend's house; she tells the mum at once and says sorry, and the mum is far less upset than if she had found it hidden.
fact: On a video call you sit facing the camera in a quiet place with good light.
fact: Before showing other people on camera, you check that they are happy to be seen.
fact: On a video call you sit still and do not eat while you talk.
fact: Before visiting a friend, you ask your parent, and an adult must know where you are and when to collect you.
fact: A guest follows the hosts' rules, even if the rules at home are different.
fact: Rooms you are not invited into and cupboards are private.
fact: Before going home you help tidy up the mess you made.
fact: If you cannot eat a food for religious, allergy or diet reasons, you say "No, thank you, I can't eat that".
fact: If a friend wants to do something dangerous or against your family's rules, you say no and can phone your parents.
fact: At a sleepover you bring your own things, follow bedtime rules and thank the family in the morning.
fact: If you break something, you tell the adult straight away and say sorry.
fact: If the family is busy or sad, you are quiet and helpful and ask if you should go home early.
exercise: You join a video call with your grandparents. Where should you sit? | Where they can see your face, in a quiet place with good light | In a dark corner of the room so that the screen looks brighter to them | Next to the TV so they can watch too | Outside on a windy street for fresh air
exercise: Your dad is in the background of your video call. What should you do before turning the camera to him? | Check that he is happy to be seen | Turn it straight to him as a surprise | Show him while he is not looking | Ask your grandparents' permission instead
exercise: What should you avoid during a video call? | Eating and walking around while you talk | Smiling at the camera | Saying hello at the start and goodbye at the end | Speaking clearly
exercise: A friend invites you to her house after school. What do you do first? | Ask your parent, so an adult knows where you are and when to collect you | Go straight there with her after school and phone home later when you remember | Tell your friend to tell your parent | Go only if it is close to your house
exercise: Your friend's family takes shoes off indoors, but you do not at home. What do you do? | Follow their rule while you are there | Keep your shoes on, since that is your rule | Ask them to change their rule for you | Take one shoe off as a compromise
exercise: You are curious about the other rooms in your friend's house. What is right? | Stay in the rooms you are invited into | Look in every room when nobody is watching | Open the cupboards to find snacks | Go into the parents' bedroom to explore
exercise: You and your friend made a big mess with toys. It is time to go. What do you do? | Help tidy up before you go | Leave it, since it is not your house | Hide the toys under the bed | Tell your friend's mum your friend did it
exercise: You are allergic to nuts and are offered a nut cake. What do you say? | "No, thank you, I can't eat that" | Eat a little so you do not seem rude | Take it and throw it away secretly | Say nothing and hope it is fine
exercise: Your friend wants to climb onto the garage roof, which your parents do not allow. What do you do? | Say no, and phone your parents if you want to go home | Climb too, because it is not your house | Climb halfway to show you are brave | Do it if your friend promises not to tell anyone, including your parents
exercise: You stay for a sleepover. Which is right? | Bring your own things, follow bedtime rules and thank the family | Stay up all night long because a sleepover is a special day with no rules | Use the family's toothbrush to save packing | Leave in the morning without a word
exercise: You break a glass at your friend's house. What do you do? | Tell the adult straight away and say sorry | Hide the pieces in the bin | Blame the family's dog | Say nothing and hope that nobody in the family ever notices
exercise: You visit, and your friend's family has just had sad news. What is thoughtful? | Be quiet and helpful and ask if you should go home early | Keep playing loudly as planned | Ask them all about the sad news so that you know what happened | Tell them to cheer up
test: A boy in Atlanta calls his aunt from a noisy, dark room while eating crisps. What should he change? | Go somewhere quiet and bright and stop eating | Turn up the volume and keep eating | Switch off the camera and talk louder so they can hear over the noise | Call from the car while driving around
test: Why must a parent know where you are and when to collect you? | So they can keep you safe | So they can plan their own day better | So the friend's family can send a bill | Because otherwise you cannot get in the door
test: At home you may jump on the sofa, but at your friend's house it is not allowed. What is right? | Follow the friend's family's rule | Jump, since your parents allow it | Jump only when the adults are out | Ask your friend to break the rule with you
test: Your friend says "Let's play with my dad's lighter!" and you feel uneasy. What do you do? | Say no, and phone your parents if you want to go home | Play for just a moment and stop as soon as it starts to get dangerous | Watch your friend do it but not touch it | Keep it secret so your friend is not in trouble
test: Why is it better to tell the family at once if you break their lamp? | Hiding it is dishonest, and the family needs to know | Because they will pay you for being honest | Because lamps can always be glued back together perfectly afterwards | Because then they cannot be cross at all
test: Your friend's cousin is filming the sleepover for a video call and points the camera at you in pyjamas. What is fair to ask? | That people check you are happy to be seen first | That they film you more often | That they share the video online so your other friends can see it too | That they film only your friend
test: A girl in Perth cannot eat pork for religious reasons, and her friend's dad serves sausages. What should she say? | "No, thank you, I can't eat that" | Nothing, and eat it anyway to be polite | "Your food is wrong" | Leave the table without a word
test: The morning after a sleepover, what should you do before you leave? | Thank the family and help tidy up | Leave quickly before breakfast | Ask to stay another night | Take some toys home as a souvenir
test: Why is it a guest's job to tidy up after playing? | It is fair to leave the house as you found it | Because hosts never tidy their own house | Because tidying up is the price you pay for being invited round | Because the toys belong to you then
test: on a vid call w nan, gonna eat my noodles while we chat | Better not eat; sit still and talk | Fine, eating shows you're relaxed and she likes seeing you eat | Fine if you point the camera away | Fine if they're not slurpy
test: going to Leo's, can i just go | Ask your parent first, so an adult knows where you are | Just go, you'll be back soon | Tell Leo, that's enough | Leave a note on the table after you've gone, that counts
test: at my mates and i broke his mums vase, oh no | Tell the adult straight away and say sorry | Hide the pieces under the bed | Blame the cat | Go home quickly
test: mate says lets ride bikes on the main road, his mum's out lol | Say no, and you can phone your parents | Go, his house means his rules, and his mum is out anyway | Go but ride fast | Go if he goes first
explain: A girl in Florida left her camera on during a video call and walked around in her pyjamas; a classmate took a screenshot and shared it, and she was embarrassed for weeks.
explain: A boy in Leeds went to a friend's house and they went off to the river without telling the parent at home; nobody knew where they were until a neighbour spotted them.
fact: Anything on a video call can be screenshotted or recorded and shared.
fact: The view from your window on a call can show where you live, so you do not show it to people you do not know well.
fact: «Her new man» means a new boyfriend, and «seeing someone» means going out with them as boyfriend or girlfriend.
fact: When a couple «split up», they stop being together.
fact: When visiting, you only go somewhere new if the adult in charge knows and agrees.
test: on a class call, gonna get changed with my camera on | Turn the camera off first, people can screenshot | Leave it on, nobody looks | Just face the wall | Only if your mic is off, then nobody can tell what you're doing
test: at my mate's, wanna go down the river, his mum's busy | Ask her first, she needs to know where you are | Go quickly and come back | Leave a note on the door | Ask your mate's little sister, she can tell their mum later
test: someone in the call wants me to show my room n window | Say no, it can show where you live | Show it, it's just a room | Show only out the window | Show it if they show theirs first, that makes it fair
test: did u hear someone screenshotted Ella in her pjs on the class call and now it's all over the whole country lol | Calls can be screenshotted, so check your camera; "whole country" is blown up | Video calls can't ever be recorded | It's funny, so you should share the screenshot too, since everyone has already seen it | Only teachers can take screenshots
test: apparently Josh n Kai went down the river from Kai's house, no one knew, a neighbour found them like miles away | An adult must know where you are first; "miles" may be exaggerated | Going is fine if you're back fast and stay close to the bank the whole time | Neighbours always find lost kids | The river is safe if two kids go
test: my mum's mate said the new boy broke a vase at Lily's n hid it, he's a total liar, pass it on | Don't spread it; the lesson is to tell the adult at once and say sorry | Pass it on so others know | Hiding broken things is clever | Tell Lily's mum the gossip yourself so that she knows what he really did
test: on the video call my cousin's like "shh, mum's on the phone with her new man, they've been talking all night lol" — what does that mean? | Her mum has a new boyfriend she talks to a lot | Her mum is talking to a builder about the house | Her mum is in trouble with the police | Her mum has a new job as a phone operator
test: at my mate's house his older bro goes "don't tell mum I'm seeing Chloe, yeah? or you're dead" — what does it mean and what's right? | He is going out with Chloe and wants it private; keep out of it, and tell an adult if the threat scares you | He watches Chloe through a window, so call the police | He'll really hurt you, so never visit again without telling anyone | Chloe is a TV show, so ask mum about it
test: visiting a friend, you overhear her parents arguing and her dad says "maybe we should just split up". she goes quiet. what's kind? | Don't ask for details or tell anyone; be a friend, and she can talk to a trusted adult | Ask her loudly if her parents hate each other | Tell everyone at school tomorrow so they're nice to her | Laugh it off, grown-ups never mean it
exam: Ascertain the conditions that must be satisfied prior to visiting a friend's house. | Your parent agrees, and an adult knows where you are and when to collect you | Your friend agrees, and no adult needs to be told about it | You leave a note, and set off without waiting for a reply | Your friend's sibling agrees, and you go after dark
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises appropriate conduct upon breaking an item at a host's house? | Tell the adult straight away and say sorry | Hide the pieces and say nothing about it | Blame the family pet for the accident | Leave at once before anyone notices it
exam: Identify the principal reason for exercising care about what is shown during a video call. | Anything on the call can be screenshotted or recorded and shared | Video calls show only faces and nothing else at all | Cameras turn off by themselves after five minutes | Only the person speaking can be seen by others
exam: It is given that a friend proposes an activity contrary to one's family rules during a visit. Determine the correct course of action. | Say no, and phone one's parents if needed | Agree, because the host's house is different | Agree, provided nobody finds out about it | Say nothing and go along with the friend
